// A list of strings is freed with its strings only when each is a string built
// for it and none was handed on. stash keeps its argument, so the piece split2
// gives it is read after split2 has returned; a literal is not a heap block at
// all and mixed puts one in a list beside a built string. Only the lists are
// freed: freeing either string would be a trap under the guard.
stash(string[] sink, string s) {
  push(sink, s);
} return void;

split2(string[] sink, int n) {
  string[] xs = [];
  push(xs, "k" + n);
  push(xs, "v" + n);
} chain (string[] sink, int n) {
  stash(sink, xs[0]);
} return void;

mixed(int n) {
  string[] ys = [];
  push(ys, "lit");
  push(ys, "m" + n);
} chain (int n) {
  print(ys[0] + ys[1]);
} return void;
